// IMPORT OF A PARENT'S NAME, REFUSED: it is a loop through containment.
//
// 'import' takes any name the registry holds, not only a module [Jon 23 Sep],
// so inside a submodule 'import Point;' names this module's public Point. But a
// parent depends on each of its children -- a program is a hierarchical
// decomposition, and a part cannot lean on the whole it is part of -- and the
// import makes the child depend on the parent: a loop of two, refused
// (ErrorImportLoop) at each import that closes one [Jon 23 Sep]. What a parent
// and its children share moves into a sister they all import.
//
// Each import is still bound, so the children's bodies resolve and the loops are
// the one thing reported. A module the parent binds by an import is a name of
// the parent like any other: inner's import of scaling, which solids
// re-exports, depends on solids and is refused too.
//
// This is the designated file of the scenario's folder, so it is the root
// module, and it is the parent both submodules below read.
//
// The folder holds:
//   scaling/scaling.cone        imports the struct, the function and the global
//                               below, and re-exports the struct
//   solids/solids.cone          imports the struct as well, and meets it a second
//                               time through a wildcard import of scaling
//   solids/inner/inner.cone     two levels down: imports from solids what solids
//                               re-exports, a module and a type

mod module_import_parent;

pub struct Point {
  pub x i64;
  pub y i64;
}

pub fn area(p Point) i64 {
  p.x * p.y;
}

pub mut count i64 = 5i64;

fn reach() i64 {
  scaling.scaledArea(3i64) + count + solids.volume(4i64) + solids.inner.deep(2i64);
}
